WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:05.465
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.465 --> 00:00:07.345
Why do you do what you do?

00:00:07.345 --> 00:00:09.643
What creates a sense of purpose for you?

00:00:09.643 --> 00:00:11.281
Is it working?

00:00:11.281 --> 00:00:13.044
Volunteering?

00:00:13.044 --> 00:00:14.787
Being with your family?

00:00:14.787 --> 00:00:16.886
These are not trivial questions.

00:00:16.886 --> 00:00:21.401
Research shows that having a strong sense
of purpose creates more meaning in life,

00:00:21.401 --> 00:00:24.830
improves health and
leads to greater satisfaction from work.

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.340
Purpose needs intention.

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.210
Having a stable far reaching goal.

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:35.380
When we're pursuing our purpose,
we feel our lives are more meaningful.

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.550
Now meaning is an abstract concept.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.460
But recently, behavioral scientists
have made progress toward

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:43.310
understanding it better and
even measuring it.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.740
I recently conducted
a study with my colleagues,

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:49.120
Roy Baumeister, Kathleen Vohs and
Emily Garbinsky, where we

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.070
asked adults to rate on a seven-point
scale whether their lives were meaningful.

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:57.400
[SOUND] We also asked about their beliefs,
behaviors, and choices.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.400
Those who deem their lives most meaningful
were those who gave the most to others.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.540
These same people also felt the most
strongly connected to the past and

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.240
future, not just the here and now.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:10.960
They had a strong sense of
where they came from, and

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:15.190
an equally strong sense of where
they are going, a purpose.

00:01:15.190 --> 00:01:18.960
So the feeling of meaning increases
when we feel connected to others and

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.840
something bigger than ourselves.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.630
When we feel we're part of a story
that started in the past and

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.230
concludes well into the future.

00:01:27.350 --> 00:01:31.050
What's more, meaning is not something
you just have or don't have.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.350
It's more about your mindset.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:37.650
When individuals adopt a meaning mindset
they behave in very different ways.

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.440
In one study two researchers,
Veronika Huta and

00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:44.640
Richard Ryan, had half of their
subjects adopt a meaning mind set by

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.690
asking them to do one thing
each day to create meaning.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:53.270
These activities, like strengthening a
social connection, pursuing excellence, or

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.520
helping others often required effort.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:00.590
Yet three months later, these subjects
reported less negative moods and

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:03.620
more positive feelings about
their social connections.

00:02:03.620 --> 00:02:05.895
Suggesting that meaning
can decrease stress and

00:02:05.895 --> 00:02:07.690
boost well-being in the long run.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:12.090
As people find more meaning in their
lives, they also tend to get healthier.

00:02:14.110 --> 00:02:17.170
Psychologist Carol Ryff and
her colleagues demonstrated this

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:20.250
in a study that tracked
thousands of people over time.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.770
The people living more meaningful lives,
by their own account, were

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:27.680
healthier than those who, while happy,
did not have the same sense of meaning.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.450
The high meaning people had higher levels
of good cholesterol, healthier weight,

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:35.700
they even slept better.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.220
They had lower levels of cortisol,
the stress hormone in their saliva.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:43.820
And were lower risk for cardiovascular
disease and Alzheimer's disease.

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:45.670
They also live longer.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.710
To figure out why, researchers had
subjects report their levels of meaning,

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:53.250
and then put the subjects
into an fMRI machine.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:56.600
Then they show them images of
negative things like crying babies or

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.110
car accidents.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:02.090
The participants then rated whether
the images were neutral or unpleasant.

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:06.730
The brain scans show that the high meaning
people had less activity in the amygdala,

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.300
the part of the brain that processes fear.

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:12.690
They also reacted more slowly
to the negative images,

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:15.609
taking longer to decide whether
the pictures were unpleasant.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:20.160
In other words, people with high
meaning in life are better able,

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:23.990
with the help of their prefrontal
cortex,to control their negative emotions.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.440
They're better able at putting
things in perspective.

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:32.170
These findings have implications for
work too.

00:03:32.170 --> 00:03:35.929
People who feel their work lacks
meaning are more likely to slack off or

00:03:35.929 --> 00:03:38.232
feel dissatisfied with their employers.

00:03:38.232 --> 00:03:41.650
The problem is, most people
feel this way about their work.

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:44.330
At least, that was the case
in one large-scale study,

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:48.980
which tracked over 12,000 employees
across a broad range of categories and

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.230
showed 50 percent lacked
a sense of meaning at work.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:56.590
The employees who did derive meaning from
their work were more than 3 times as

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:01.290
likely to stay with their companies
They also reported 1.7 times higher job

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:05.460
satisfaction, and
were 1.4 times more engaged at work.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:10.270
Which brings us back to the original
question: why do you do what you do?

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:15.950
If you work only for the money, chances
are you're less effective than you can be.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.220
But if your answer entails meaning,

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:22.390
your work help should become part of a
bigger story and feel connected to others,

00:04:22.390 --> 00:04:26.215
then it will help you be stronger,
healthier and more resilient.

00:04:26.215 --> 00:04:28.674
[SOUND]

